d Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Source Code pure subroutine dense_lu_solve ( a , piv , b ) real ( wp ), dimension (:,:), intent ( in ) :: a !! LU factors integer , dimension (:), intent ( in ) :: piv !! row pivots real ( wp ), dimension (:), intent ( inout ) :: b !! right-hand side, overwritten by the solution integer :: i , k2 k2 = size ( a , 1 ) do i = 1 , k2 if ( piv ( i ) /= i ) b ([ i , piv ( i )]) = b ([ piv ( i ), i ]) end do do i = 2 , k2 b ( i ) = b ( i ) - dot_product ( a ( i , 1 : i - 1 ), b ( 1 : i - 1 )) end do do i = k2 , 1 , - 1 b ( i ) = ( b ( i ) - dot_product ( a ( i , i + 1 : k2 ), b ( i + 1 : k2 ))) / a ( i , i ) end do end subroutine dense_lu_solve","tags":"","url":"proc/dense_lu_solve.html"},{"title":"dense_symmetric_inertia – sqpopt","text":"public pure subroutine dense_symmetric_inertia(a, n_positive, n_negative, n_zero, zero_tol) the inertia of the symmetric matrix a : its numbers of positive,\nnegative, and zero eigenvalues. The matrix is reduced to tridiagonal form\nby Householder reflections (a congruence with an orthogonal matrix, so\nthe eigenvalues are unchanged), and the signs are counted from the\npivots of that form's factorization (its Sturm sequence at\nzero; Golub & Van Loan, Matrix Computations , 8.3 and 8.4), which has\nthe same inertia as the matrix (Sylvester's law). A pivot counts as zero\nif it is below zero_tol (default ) times the matrix's\nlargest element. A zero\npivot that is coupled to the next row is not a zero eigenvalue (the\nmatrix has the eigenvalues ): it is taken\nwith that row as a 2 by 2 pivot, as in the Bunch-Kaufman factorization,\nand the block's two eigenvalues are counted. Costs : meant\nfor small matrices. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:,:) :: a the symmetric matrix dimension(n,n) integer, intent(out) :: n_positive number of positive eigenvalues integer, intent(out) :: n_negative number of negative eigenvalues integer, intent(out) :: n_zero number of zero eigenvalues real(kind=wp), intent(in), optional :: zero_tol relative tolerance of a zero pivot (default 1e-12 ) Called by proc~~dense_symmetric_inertia~~CalledByGraph proc~dense_symmetric_inertia dense_symmetric_inertia proc~dense_refactor sqpopt_dense_ldl_type%dense_refactor proc~dense_refactor->proc~dense_symmetric_inertia proc~low_rank_update low_rank_update proc~low_rank_update->proc~dense_symmetric_inertia proc~kkt_factor sqpopt_kkt_type%kkt_factor proc~kkt_factor->proc~low_rank_update proc~direct_qp_step direct_qp_step proc~direct_qp_step->proc~kkt_factor proc~inertia_correct sqpopt_inertia_type%inertia_correct proc~direct_qp_step->proc~inertia_correct proc~inertia_correct->proc~kkt_factor proc~solve_qp_subproblem sqpopt_qp_solver_type%solve_qp_subproblem proc~solve_qp_subproblem->proc~direct_qp_step proc~sqpopt_iterate sqpopt_iterate proc~sqpopt_iterate->proc~inertia_correct proc~sqpopt_iterate->proc~solve_qp_subproblem proc~restoration_phase_step sqpopt_restoration_type%restoration_phase_step proc~sqpopt_iterate->proc~restoration_phase_step proc~trust_region_step sqpopt_trust_region_type%trust_region_step proc~sqpopt_iterate->proc~trust_region_step proc~restoration_phase_step->proc~solve_qp_subproblem proc~sqpopt_solve sqpopt_type%sqpopt_solve proc~sqpopt_solve->proc~sqpopt_iterate proc~trust_region_step->proc~solve_qp_subproblem proc~nlls_solve sqpopt_nlls_type%nlls_solve proc~nlls_solve->proc~sqpopt_solve Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Source Code pure subroutine dense_symmetric_inertia ( a , n_positive , n_negative , n_zero , zero_tol ) real ( wp ), dimension (:,:), intent ( in ) :: a !! the symmetric matrix `dimension(n,n)` integer , intent ( out ) :: n_positive !! number of positive eigenvalues integer , intent ( out ) :: n_negative !! number of negative eigenvalues integer , intent ( out ) :: n_zero !! number of zero eigenvalues real ( wp ), optional , intent ( in ) :: zero_tol !! relative tolerance of a zero pivot (default `1e-12`) real ( wp ), parameter :: default_zero_tol = 1.0e-12_wp real ( wp ) :: tol real ( wp ), dimension (:,:), allocatable :: b real ( wp ), dimension (:), allocatable :: v , p , w real ( wp ) :: alpha , vnorm , q , small , e , d , det , mean , radius integer :: n , k , i allocate ( b ( size ( a , 1 ), size ( a , 1 )), v ( size ( a , 1 )), p ( size ( a , 1 )), w ( size ( a , 1 ))) n = size ( a , 1 ) n_positive = 0 n_negative = 0 n_zero = 0 if ( n == 0 ) return b = 0.5_wp * ( a + transpose ( a )) tol = default_zero_tol if ( present ( zero_tol )) tol = zero_tol small = tol * max ( maxval ( abs ( b )), tiny ( 1.0_wp )) ! Householder reduction to tridiagonal form: do k = 1 , n - 2 alpha = norm2 ( b ( k + 1 : n , k )) if ( alpha <= 0.0_wp ) cycle if ( b ( k + 1 , k ) > 0.0_wp ) alpha = - alpha v ( k + 1 : n ) = b ( k + 1 : n , k ) v ( k + 1 ) = v ( k + 1 ) - alpha vnorm = norm2 ( v ( k + 1 : n )) if ( vnorm <= 0.0_wp ) cycle v ( k + 1 : n ) = v ( k + 1 : n ) / vnorm ! B <- (I - 2vv&#94;T) B (I - 2vv&#94;T) on the trailing block: p ( k + 1 : n ) = matmul ( b ( k + 1 : n , k + 1 : n ), v ( k + 1 : n )) w ( k + 1 : n ) = p ( k + 1 : n ) - dot_product ( v ( k + 1 : n ), p ( k + 1 : n )) * v ( k + 1 : n ) do i = k + 1 , n b ( k + 1 : n , i ) = b ( k + 1 : n , i ) - 2.0_wp * ( v ( k + 1 : n ) * w ( i ) + w ( k + 1 : n ) * v ( i )) end do b ( k + 1 , k ) = alpha b ( k , k + 1 ) = alpha b ( k + 2 : n , k ) = 0.0_wp b ( k , k + 2 : n ) = 0.0_wp end do ! the signs of the pivots of the tridiagonal matrix (`q` is the pivot of ! row `i`: its diagonal element, less the effect of the rows above): q = b ( 1 , 1 ) i = 1 do while ( i <= n ) if ( i < n ) then e = b ( i + 1 , i ) d = b ( i + 1 , i + 1 ) else e = 0.0_wp d = 0.0_wp end if if ( abs ( q ) >= small ) then ! a 1 by 1 pivot: call count ( q , n_positive , n_negative , n_zero ) q = d - e ** 2 / q i = i + 1 else if ( abs ( e ) < small ) then ! a zero pivot that the next row doesn't depend on: a zero eigenvalue call count ( q , n_positive , n_negative , n_zero ) q = d i = i + 1 else ! a zero pivot, coupled to the next row: a 2 by 2 pivot [q, e; e, d], ! with the eigenvalues mean +/- radius mean = 0.5_wp * ( q + d ) radius = hypot ( 0.5_wp * ( q - d ), e ) call count ( mean + radius , n_positive , n_negative , n_zero ) call count ( mean - radius , n_positive , n_negative , n_zero ) if ( i + 2 <= n ) then ! the next pivot: the block's inverse has q/det in its last element det = q * d - e ** 2 if ( det /= 0.0_wp ) then q = b ( i + 2 , i + 2 ) - b ( i + 2 , i + 1 ) ** 2 * q / det else q = b ( i + 2 , i + 2 ) end if end if i = i + 2 end if end do contains pure subroutine count ( eigenvalue , n_pos , n_neg , n_null ) !! add an eigenvalue (or a pivot) to the count of its sign real ( wp ), intent ( in ) :: eigenvalue !! the eigenvalue integer , intent ( inout ) :: n_pos !! number of positive eigenvalues so far integer , intent ( inout ) :: n_neg !! number of negative eigenvalues so far integer , intent ( inout ) :: n_null !! number of zero eigenvalues so far if ( abs ( eigenvalue ) < small ) then n_null = n_null + 1 else if ( eigenvalue > 0.0_wp ) then n_pos = n_pos + 1 else n_neg = n_neg + 1 end if end subroutine count end subroutine dense_symmetric_inertia","tags":"","url":"proc/dense_symmetric_inertia.html"},{"title":"soc_step – sqpopt","text":"public  subroutine soc_step(jac, x, p, c, c_trial, c_lb, c_ub, x_lb, x_ub, p_soc, ok, least_squares) compute the second-order-corrected step p_soc for the trial step p (see the module-level documentation). ok is false (and p_soc=p ) if\nthere is nothing to correct or the correction is not usable. With least_squares (if it is enabled), the correction is computed by a\ndirect solve, and by LSQR only if that fails. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( sqpopt_sparse_matrix ), intent(in) :: jac constraint Jacobian at x , dimension(m,n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: x current point dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: p the rejected trial step dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: c constraint values at x dimension(m) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: c_trial constraint values at x+p dimension(m) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: c_lb constraint lower bounds dimension(m) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: c_ub constraint upper bounds dimension(m) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: x_lb variable lower bounds dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: x_ub variable upper bounds dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(out), dimension(:) :: p_soc corrected step dimension(n) logical, intent(out) :: ok true if p_soc is a usable corrected step type( sqpopt_least_squares_type ), intent(inout), optional :: least_squares the direct least-squares solver\n(see sqpopt_least_squares_module ) Calls proc~~soc_step~~CallsGraph proc~soc_step soc_step initialize initialize proc~soc_step->initialize proc~least_squares_min_norm sqpopt_least_squares_type%least_squares_min_norm proc~soc_step->proc~least_squares_min_norm proc~sparse_matvec sparse_matvec proc~soc_step->proc~sparse_matv