lass ( sqpopt_merit_type ), intent ( in ) :: me real ( wp ), dimension (:), intent ( in ) :: c !! constraint values at `x` `dimension(m)` real ( wp ), dimension (:), intent ( in ) :: c_lb !! lower bounds on the constraints `dimension(m)` real ( wp ), dimension (:), intent ( in ) :: c_ub !! upper bounds on the constraints `dimension(m)` real ( wp ), dimension (:), intent ( in ) :: lambda !! Lagrange multipliers for the constraints `dimension(m)` real ( wp ), dimension (:), intent ( out ) :: s !! closed-form slack that minimizes the augmented Lagrangian merit function if ( me % penalty <= 0.0_wp ) then s = min ( max ( c , c_lb ), c_ub ) else s = min ( max ( c - lambda / me % penalty , c_lb ), c_ub ) end if end subroutine augmented_lagrangian_slacks","tags":"","url":"proc/augmented_lagrangian_slacks.html"},{"title":"merit_directional_derivative – sqpopt","text":"private  subroutine merit_directional_derivative(me, jac, g, p, c, c_lb, c_ub, lambda, dphi0) the (approximate) directional derivative of the merit\nfunction along p , used by the Armijo sufficient-decrease test and by\nthe descent-direction safeguard in sqpopt_iterate_module . Dispatches\non me%mode : sqpopt_merit_l1 : , the exact one-sided derivative of the\n  linearized violation. For a step that satisfies the linearized\n  constraints this is the usual ; for one that doesn't (e.g. shortened by the step-length\n  cap, or an elastic step), that formula would overstate the decrease,\n  so that no step length could pass the sufficient-decrease test. sqpopt_merit_augmented_lagrangian : , holding and s fixed at their\n  current values; with the joint step ( joint_active , see update_penalty_parameter ), where changes by and by per unit step, it is . Type Bound sqpopt_merit_type Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( sqpopt_merit_type ), intent(in) :: me type( sqpopt_sparse_matrix ), intent(in) :: jac constraint Jacobian at x , dimension(m,n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: g objective gr