ule~sqpopt_qp_direct_module->module~sqpopt_inertia_module module~sqpopt_qp_solver_module->module~sqpopt_kkt_module module~sqpopt_qp_solver_module->module~sqpopt_inertia_module module~sqpopt_qp_solver_module->module~sqpopt_qp_direct_module module~sqpopt_trust_region_module->module~sqpopt_kkt_module module~sqpopt_trust_region_module->module~sqpopt_inertia_module module~sqpopt_trust_region_module->module~sqpopt_least_squares_module module~sqpopt_trust_region_module->module~sqpopt_qp_solver_module module~sqpopt_trust_region_module->module~sqpopt_soc_module module~sqpopt_restoration_module->module~sqpopt_least_squares_module module~sqpopt_restoration_module->module~sqpopt_qp_solver_module module~sqpopt_soc_module->module~sqpopt_least_squares_module Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Abstract Interfaces abstract interface private  subroutine start_interface(me, n, irow, icol, ok, threads, signs) start the backend for symmetric matrices of order n with the\nsparsity pattern irow / icol (each entry stands for itself and,\noff the diagonal, its mirror image; entries given more than once\nare added together). ok is false if it couldn't be started. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( sqpopt_sparse_ldl_type ), intent(inout) :: me integer, intent(in) :: n order of the matrix integer, intent(in), dimension(:) :: irow row indices of the entries dimension(nnz) integer, intent(in), dimension(:) :: icol column indices of the entries dimension(nnz) logical, intent(out) :: ok whether the backend is ready integer, intent(in), optional :: threads number of OpenMP threads, if the backend uses them integer, intent(in), optional, dimension(:) :: signs the expected sign of each row's pivot ( -1 , 0 , or +1 ), if the backend uses it dimension(n) abstract interface private  subroutine refactor_interface(me, val, ok) factor the matrix with the values val (in the order of the\npattern), and set n_negative , n_null , and inertia_known Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( sqpopt_sparse_ldl_type ), intent(inout) :: me real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: val values of the entries dimension(nnz) logical, intent(out) :: ok whether the factorization succeeded abstract interface private  subroutine back_solve_interface(me, v, ok) one solve with the factors, without refinement: v is overwritten\nby (if not ok , v is unchanged) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( sqpopt_sparse_ldl_type ), intent(inout) :: me real(kind=wp), intent(inout), dimension(:) :: v the right-hand side, overwritten by the solution logical, intent(out) :: ok whether it was solved abstract interface private  subroutine multiply_interface(me, x, y) the product with the values last factored Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( sqpopt_sparse_ldl_type ), intent(in) :: me real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: x the vector dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(out), dimension(:) :: y the product dimension(n) abstract interface private  subroutine free_interface(me) free everything the backend holds Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( sqpopt_sparse_ldl_type ), intent(inout) :: me Derived Types type, public, abstract :: sqpopt_sparse_ldl_type a sparse symmetric backend, for matrices with one\nsparsity pattern (see the module documentation) Components Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, public :: n_negative = 0 of the last factorization: the number of negative eigenvalues integer, public :: n_null = 0 of the last factorization: the number of zero eigenvalues logical, public :: inertia_known = .true. of the last factorization: whether n_negative and n_null are\nthe matrix's (else they are those of the factors, which are not\nthe matrix's: see the backend's documentation) logical, public :: out_of_memory = .false. whether an allocation of the backend failed (it stays set) Type-Bound Procedures procedure( start_interface ), public, deferred :: start analyse the sparsity pattern procedure( refactor_interface ), public, deferred :: refactor factor new values procedure( back_solve_interface ), public, deferred :: back_solve one solve with the factors procedure( multiply_interface ), public, deferred :: multiply the product with the last values procedure( free_interface ), public, deferred :: free free everything","tags":"","url":"module/sqpopt_sparse_ldl_module.html"},{"title":"sqpopt_dense_ldl_module – sqpopt","text":"The dense backend of sqpopt_symmetric_solver_module ( options%linear_solver = sqpopt_linear_solver_dense ), for small\nmatrices: the matrix is stored dense (order n , so elements),\nits inertia is the exact count of dense_symmetric_inertia (Householder tridiagonalization, then the signs of a Sturm sequence, with\n2 by 2 pivots for coupled zeros, as Bunch-Kaufman's), and solves use an\nLU factorization with partial pivoting. So, unlike QDLDL, it always\nknows the inertia, also of a Hessian with zeros on its diagonal, and,\nunlike MUMPS, it has almost no overhead per call. But each factorization\ncosts : it is meant for matrices of order up to a few hundred,\nand refuses to start above dense_max_order (the factorization options\nthen fall back on the matrix-free methods). A singular matrix is not an error: its zero eigenvalues are counted\n( n_null ), and in the LU factorization a null column gets a large\npivot, which makes that component of a solution zero, so that a solve\nreturns one of the solutions of a consistent system (as MUMPS's\nnull-pivot detection does). Uses sqpopt_types_module sqpopt_sparse_ldl_module sqpopt_kinds sqpopt_dense_linalg_module module~~sqpopt_dense_ldl_module~~UsesGraph module~sqpopt_dense_ldl_mod