sqpopt_qp_solver_module->module~sqpopt_kkt_module module~sqpopt_qp_solver_module->module~sqpopt_qp_dense_module module~sqpopt_qp_solver_module->module~sqpopt_inertia_module module~sqpopt_qp_solver_module->module~sqpopt_qp_direct_module module~sqpopt_qp_solver_module->module~sqpopt_qp_reduced_hessian_module module~sqpopt_restoration_module->module~sqpopt_hessian_module module~sqpopt_restoration_module->module~sqpopt_least_squares_module module~sqpopt_restoration_module->module~sqpopt_qp_solver_module module~sqpopt_symmetric_solver_module->module~sqpopt_dense_ldl_module module~sqpopt_trust_region_module->module~sqpopt_hessian_module module~sqpopt_trust_region_module->module~sqpopt_kkt_module module~sqpopt_trust_region_module->module~sqpopt_inertia_module module~sqpopt_trust_region_module->module~sqpopt_least_squares_module module~sqpopt_trust_region_module->module~sqpopt_qp_solver_module module~sqpopt_trust_region_module->module~sqpopt_soc_module module~sqpopt_diagnostics_module->module~sqpopt_options_module module~sqpopt_diagnostics_module->module~sqpopt_qp_solver_module module~sqpopt_soc_module->module~sqpopt_least_squares_module Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Subroutines public  subroutine dense_null_space (a, m_a, n, z, n_z) compute an orthonormal basis Z ( dimension(n,n_z) ) for the null\n space of A ( dimension(m_a,n) , m_a<=n assumed full row rank), via\n Householder QR of : , so null(A) is spanned by\n the last n-m_a columns of Q . Z is allocated here ( intent(out),\n allocatable ) with dimension(n,n_z) , n_z = n-m_a . Read more… Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:,:) :: a dimension(m_a,n) integer, intent(in) :: m_a number of rows of a (may be 0) integer, intent(in) :: n number of columns of a (and rows of z ) real(kind=wp), intent(out), dimension(:,:), allocatable :: z dimension(n,n_z) , n_z = n-m_a integer, intent(out) :: n_z number of columns of z public  subroutine dense_modified_cholesky (a, n, l) modified Cholesky factorization of a symmetric dimension(n,n) matrix a that is not guaranteed positive definite\n(the limited-memory BFGS/SR1 Hessian restricted to a subspace has no\nsuch guarantee). Uses a simple Gill-Murray-Wright-style safeguard: any\npivot that would be non-positive is instead replaced by a small\npositive floor, so the factorization always succeeds and returns a\n(perturbed) positive-definite L . Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(n,n) :: a symmetric matrix to be factorized integer, intent(in) :: n order of the matrix a real(kind=wp), intent(out), dimension(n,n) :: l lower-triangular Cholesky factor of a public  subroutine dense_cholesky_curvature (a, n, l, ok, d) Cholesky factorization of a symmetric dimension(n,n) matrix a that may not be positive definite, without perturbing it.\nIf a is (numerically) positive definite, ok=.true. and l is its\nCholesky factor. Otherwise the factorization stops at the first column j whose pivot is not\nsufficiently positive (relative to the largest diagonal element), and\nreturns ok=.false. with a direction d of nonpositive curvature, (roughly) 0: for which equals that pivot (the Schur complement). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(n,n) :: a symmetric matrix to be factorized integer, intent(in) :: n order of the matrix a real(kind=wp), intent(out), dimension(n,n) :: l lower-triangular Cholesky factor (valid only if ok ) logical, intent(out) :: ok true if a is positive definite real(kind=wp), intent(out), dimension(n) :: d direction of nonpositive curvature (only if .not. ok ) public  subroutine dense_solve_cholesky (l, n, b, x) solve given the Cholesky factor l ( , from dense_modified_cholesky ) via forward and back substitution. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(n,n) :: l lower-triangular Cholesky factor of a integer, intent(in) :: n order of the matrix l real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(n) :: b right-hand side vector of the linear system Ax=b real(kind=wp), intent(out), dimension(n) :: x solution vector of the linear system Ax=b public pure subroutine dense_lu_factor (a, piv, ok) in-place LU factorization with partial pivoting of a small dense\nmatrix a (e.g. the middle matrix of sqpopt_hessian_module , whose order is 2*max_history at most, independent of n ). ok is false if a pivot is negligible relative to the matrix's largest\nelement. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(inout), dimension(:,:) :: a matrix, overwritten by its L (unit, below the diagonal) and U factors integer, intent(out), dimension(:) :: piv piv(p) is the row swapped with row p at step p logical, intent(out) :: ok false if a is (numerically) singular public pure subroutine dense_lu_solve (a, piv, b) solve in place ( b is overwritten by x ), given the LU\nfactors from dense_lu_factor . Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:,:) :: a LU factors integer, intent(in), dimension(:) :: piv row pivots real(kind=wp), intent(inout), dimension(:) :: b right-hand side, overwritten by the solution public pure subroutine dense_symmetric_inertia (a, n_positive, n_negative, n_zero, zero_tol) the inertia of the symmetric matrix a : its numbers of positive,\nnegative, and zero eigenvalues. The matrix is reduced to tridiagonal form\nby Householder reflections (a congruence with an orthogonal matrix, so\nthe eigenvalues are unchanged), and the signs are counted from the\npivots of that form's factorization (its Sturm sequence at\nzero; Golub & Van Loan, Matrix Computations , 8.3 and 8.4), which has\nthe same inertia as the matrix (Sylvester's law). A pivot counts as zero\nif it is below zero_tol (default ) times the matrix's\nlargest element. A zero\npivot that is coupled to the next row is not a zero eigenvalue (the\nmatrix has the eigenvalues ): it is taken\nwith that row as a 2 by 2 pivot, as in the Bunch-Kaufman factorization,\nand the block's two eigenvalues are counted. Costs : meant\nfor small matrices. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:,:) :: a the symmetric matrix dimension(n,n) integer, intent(out) :: n_positive number of positive eigenvalues integer, intent(out) :: n_negative number of negative eigenvalues integer, intent(out) :: n_zero number of zero eigenvalues real(kind=wp), intent(in), optional :: zero_tol relative tolerance of a zero pivot (default 1e-12 )","tags":"","url":"module/sqpopt_dense_linalg_module.html"},{"title":"sqpopt_soc_module – sqpopt","text":"Second-order correction (SOC) step, used by the line searches in sqpopt_linesearch_module and by the trust-region step in sqpopt_trust_region_module when a full step has been rejected. For strongly nonlinear constraints, a genuinely good step p can be\n  rejected by the merit function or filter because the linearized constraint prediction differs from the true value (the Maratos effect). The SOC step adds a small\n  correction d that accounts for the true constraint values at x+p ,\n  reusing the Jacobian at x (no new derivative evaluations): solved in the minimum-norm sense with LSQR (or directly, with options%direct_least_squares : see sqpopt_least_squares_module ).\n  Here S is the set of\n  constraints that are active in the linearization ( at a bound b ), or violated at x+p (with b the violated bound). The corrected\n  step p+d is then projected onto the variable bounds. The correction is\n  not used if it is larger than the step itself (it is meant to be a small\n  correction; a large one comes from a nearly singular ), or if LSQR stops at its iteration limit. Note Variables at a bound at x+p are not held fixed in the solve for d , so the final projection can undo part of the correction. (Holding\n      them fixed, or re-solving with the variables that the correction takes\n      outside their bounds held fixed, was tried: it saves function\n      evaluations on some problems, but makes the correction large where\n      the free variables' columns of are small, e.g. TP13, and\n      sends TP116 to a different local solution.) Uses lsqr_module sqpopt_linalg_module sqpopt_types_module sqpopt_least_squares_module sqpopt_kinds module~~sqpopt_soc_module~~UsesGraph module~sqpopt_soc_module sqpopt_soc_module lsqr_module lsqr_module module~sqpopt_soc_module->lsqr_module module~sqpopt_kinds sqpopt_kinds module~sqpopt_soc_module->module~sqpopt_kinds module~sqpopt_least_squares_module sqpopt_least_squares_module module~sqpopt_soc_module->module~sqpopt_least_squares_module module~sqpopt_linalg_module sqpopt_linalg_module module~sqpopt_soc_module->module~sqpopt_linalg_module module~sqpopt_types_module sqpopt_types_module module~sqpopt_soc_module->module~sqpopt_types_module iso_fortran_env iso_fortran_env module~sqpopt_kinds->iso_fortran_env module~sqpopt_least_squares_module->lsqr_module module~sqpopt_least_squares_module->module~sqpopt_kinds module~sqpopt_least_squares_module->module~sqpopt_types_module module~sqpopt_kkt_module sqpopt_kkt_module module~sqpopt_least_squares_module->module~sqpopt_kkt_module module~sqpopt_linalg_module->module~sqpopt_kinds module~sqpopt_linalg_module->module~sqpopt_types_module lusol lusol module~sqpopt_linalg_module->lusol lusol_precision lusol_precision module~sqpopt_linalg_module->lusol_precision module~sqpopt_types_module->module~sqpopt_kinds ieee_arithmetic ieee_arithmetic module~sqpopt_types_module->ieee_arithmetic module~sqpopt_kkt_module->module~sqpopt_kinds module~sqpopt_kkt_module->module~sqpopt_types_module module~sqpopt_dense_linalg_