point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Used by module~~sqpopt_soc_module~~UsedByGraph module~sqpopt_soc_module sqpopt_soc_module module~sqpopt_iterate_module sqpopt_iterate_module module~sqpopt_iterate_module->module~sqpopt_soc_module module~sqpopt_trust_region_module sqpopt_trust_region_module module~sqpopt_iterate_module->module~sqpopt_trust_region_module module~sqpopt_trust_region_module->module~sqpopt_soc_module module~sqpopt_module sqpopt_module module~sqpopt_module->module~sqpopt_iterate_module module~sqpopt_module->module~sqpopt_trust_region_module module~sqpopt_nlls_module sqpopt_nlls_module module~sqpopt_nlls_module->module~sqpopt_trust_region_module module~sqpopt_nlls_module->module~sqpopt_module Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Subroutines public  subroutine soc_step (jac, x, p, c, c_trial, c_lb, c_ub, x_lb, x_ub, p_soc, ok, least_squares) compute the second-order-corrected step p_soc for the trial step p (see the module-level documentation). ok is false (and p_soc=p ) if\nthere is nothing to correct or the correction is not usable. With least_squares (if it is enabled), the correction is computed by a\ndirect solve, and by LSQR only if that fails. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( sqpopt_sparse_matrix ), intent(in) :: jac constraint Jacobian at x , dimension(m,n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: x current point dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: p the rejected trial step dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: c constraint values at x dimension(m) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: c_trial constraint values at x+p dimension(m) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: c_lb constraint lower bounds dimension(m) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: c_ub constraint upper bounds dimension(m) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: x_lb variable lower bounds dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: x_ub variable upper bounds dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(out), dimension(:) :: p_soc corrected step dimension(n) logical, intent(out) :: ok true if p_soc is a usable corrected step type( sqpopt_least_squares_type ), intent(inout), optional :: least_squares the direct least-squares solver\n(see sqpopt_least_squares_module )","tags":"","url":"module/sqpopt_soc_module.html"},{"title":"sqpopt_symmetric_solver_module – sqpopt","text":"A direct solver for symmetric indefinite linear systems , with one of three backends\n( options%linear_solver ), each in a module of its own, behind the\ninterface of sqpopt_sparse_ldl_module : sqpopt_linear_solver_qdldl (the default, in every build): QDLDL\n  ( sqpopt_qdldl_ldl_module ), without pivoting, single-threaded, with\n  very little overhead, always available; sqpopt_linear_solver_mumps : MUMPS ( sqpopt_mumps_ldl_module ), with\n  pivoting and threads, in a library built with it ( sqpopt_has_mumps ); sqpopt_linear_solver_dense (opt-in): the matrix as a dense array\n  ( sqpopt_dense_ldl_module ), with pivoting and the exact inertia, for\n  small matrices only ( per factorization; it refuses an\n  order above dense_max_order ). This module chooses the backend, and does what is the same for both:\nthe iterative refinement of the solves, the counts, and the timing. Which one. Measured (release; see the user guide's \"Sparse solver\"\nsection): on the banded and chained problems of example/benchmark_large.f90 (5,000 to 1,000,000 variables) both take\nthe same iterations, and QDLDL's factorizations take 7 to 30 times less\ntime; on the Hock-Schittkowski problems the quasi-Newton Hessians give\nthe same results with both (QDLDL's faster), but the exact Hessian with\ninertia control needs about 45% more evaluations with QDLDL, which can't\ntell the inertia of a Hessian with zeros on its diagonal (see inertia_known ). On grids ( example/sparse_solvers.f90 ), MUMPS\nrefactors 2 to 10 times faster in 2-D and 9 to 34 times in 3-D, where\nthe factors have large dense blocks (and its threads help), though its\nfirst factorization, with its analysis, can cost much more (72 s against\n4.5 s for a 2-D KKT matrix of order 735,000). So MUMPS is the better\nchoice for the exact Hessian with inertia control, and for problems\ncoupled in two or three dimensions that refactor many times. For a small\nproblem with the exact Hessian and inertia control, the dense backend\ngives MUMPS's results (on the Hock-Schittkowski problems, 9,452\nevaluations against MUMPS's 9,400 and QDLDL's 13,654) at QDLDL's speed. How it is used: symmetric_solver_initialize with the sparsity pattern (the entries\n   of one triangle, in coordinate form; entries given more than once are\n   added together). QDLDL analyses (orders) it here, MUMPS in the first\n   factorization; either way only once, so a matrix whose values change\n   but whose pattern doesn't is cheap to refactor. symmetric_solver_factor with the values of those entries. The\n   factorization gives the inertia of the matrix: n_negative negative and n_null zero eigenvalues, if inertia_known (else those\n   are the counts of the factors, which are not the matrix's). symmetric_solver_solve , any number of times, for right-hand sides.\n   Each solution is improved by iterative refinement. symmetric_solver_destroy to free it. A backend may hold pointers\n   (MUMPS), so it must not be copied. A singular matrix is not an error: its null pivots are counted\n( n_null ), and a solve then returns one of the solutions of a consistent\nsystem. Uses sqpopt_sparse_ldl_module sqpopt_qdldl_ldl_module sqpopt_dense_ldl_module sqpopt_types_module sqpopt_mumps_ldl_module iso_fortran_env sqpopt_kinds module~~sqpopt_symmetric_solver_module~~UsesGraph module~sqpopt_symmetric_solver_module sqpopt_symmetric_solver_module iso_fortran_env iso_fortran_env module~sqpopt_symmetric_solver_module->iso_fortran_env module~sqpopt_dense_ldl_module sqpopt_dense_ldl_module module~sqpopt_symmetric_solver_module->module~sqpopt_dense_ldl_module module~sqpopt_kinds sqpopt_kinds module~sqpopt_symmetric_solver_module->module~sqpopt_kinds module~sqpopt_mumps_ldl_module sqpopt_mumps_ldl_module module~sqpopt_symmetric_solver_module->module~sqpopt_mumps_ldl_module module~sqpopt_qdldl_ldl_module sqpopt_qdldl_ldl_module module~sqpopt_symmetric_solver_module->module~sqpopt_qdldl_ldl_module module~sqpopt_sparse_ldl_module sqpopt_sparse_ldl_module module~sqpopt_symmetric_solver_module->module~sqpopt_sparse_ldl_module module~sqpopt_types_module sqpopt_types_module module~sqpopt_symmetric_solver_module->module~sqpopt_types_module module~sqpopt_dense_ldl_module->module~sqpopt_kinds module~sqpopt_dense_ldl_module->module~sqpopt_sparse_ldl_module module~sqpopt_dense_ldl_module->module~sqpopt_types_module module~sqpopt_dense_linalg_module sqpopt_dense_linalg_module module~sqpopt_dense_ldl_module->module~sqpopt_dense_linalg_module module~sqpopt_kinds->iso_fortran_env module~sqpopt_mumps_ldl_module->module~sqpopt_kinds module~sqpopt_mumps_ldl_module->module~sqpopt_sparse_ldl_module module~sqpopt_qdldl_ldl_module->module~sqpopt_kinds module~sqpopt_qdldl_ldl_module->module~sqpopt_sparse_ldl_module qdldl_module qdldl_module module~sqpopt_qdldl_ldl_module->qdldl_module module~sqpopt_sparse_ldl_module->module~sqpopt_kinds module~sqpopt_types_module->module~sqpopt_kinds ieee_arithmetic ieee_arithmetic module~sqpopt_types_module->ieee_arithmetic module~sqpopt_dense_linalg_module->module~sqpopt_kinds Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subrouti