rate_module->module~sqpopt_diagnostics_module module~sqpopt_iterate_module->module~sqpopt_inertia_module module~sqpopt_iterate_module->module~sqpopt_least_squares_module module~sqpopt_qp_direct_module sqpopt_qp_direct_module module~sqpopt_iterate_module->module~sqpopt_qp_direct_module module~sqpopt_iterate_module->module~sqpopt_qp_solver_module module~sqpopt_iterate_module->module~sqpopt_trust_region_module module~sqpopt_iterate_module->module~sqpopt_restoration_module module~sqpopt_soc_module sqpopt_soc_module module~sqpopt_iterate_module->module~sqpopt_soc_module module~sqpopt_least_squares_module->module~sqpopt_kkt_module module~sqpopt_nlls_module sqpopt_nlls_module module~sqpopt_nlls_module->module~sqpopt_module module~sqpopt_nlls_module->module~sqpopt_options_module module~sqpopt_nlls_module->module~sqpopt_qp_solver_module module~sqpopt_nlls_module->module~sqpopt_trust_region_module module~sqpopt_qp_direct_module->module~sqpopt_kkt_module module~sqpopt_qp_direct_module->module~sqpopt_inertia_module module~sqpopt_qp_solver_module->module~sqpopt_kkt_module module~sqpopt_qp_solver_module->module~sqpopt_inertia_module module~sqpopt_qp_solver_module->module~sqpopt_qp_direct_module module~sqpopt_trust_region_module->module~sqpopt_kkt_module module~sqpopt_trust_region_module->module~sqpopt_inertia_module module~sqpopt_trust_region_module->module~sqpopt_least_squares_module module~sqpopt_trust_region_module->module~sqpopt_qp_solver_module module~sqpopt_trust_region_module->module~sqpopt_soc_module module~sqpopt_restoration_module->module~sqpopt_least_squares_module module~sqpopt_restoration_module->module~sqpopt_qp_solver_module module~sqpopt_soc_module->module~sqpopt_least_squares_module Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, public, parameter :: sqpopt_linear_solver_mumps = 1 MUMPS (needs a library built with it) integer, public, parameter :: sqpopt_linear_solver_qdldl = 2 (default) QDLDL (always available; see the\nmodule documentation) integer, public, parameter :: sqpopt_linear_solver_dense = 3 dense, for small matrices (always available;\nsee sqpopt_dense_ldl_module ) Derived Types type, public :: sqpopt_symmetric_solver_type a sparse symmetric indefinite solver for matrices with one sparsity\npattern (see the module documentation). Its backend may hold\npointers, so it must not be copied, and symmetric_solver_destroy must be called when it is no longer needed. Components Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial logical, public :: ready = .false. whether it is initialized logical, public :: factored = .false. whether it holds a factorization (to solve with) integer, public :: n_negative = 0 of the last factorization: the number of negative eigenvalues integer, public :: n_null = 0 of the last factorization: the number of zero eigenvalues logical, public :: inertia_known = .true. of the last factorization: whether n_negative and n_null are the matrix's (a backend without pivoting may not be able\nto tell: see sqpopt_qdldl_ldl_module ) integer, public :: n_factor = 0 number of factorizations so far integer, public :: n_solve = 0 number of solves so far (including those of the refinement) real(kind=wp), public :: time = 0.0_wp wall-clock time spent in the solver so far (seconds) logical, public :: out_of_memory = .false. whether an analysis, factorization, or solve ran out of\nmemory (it stays set) integer, public :: backend = 0 the solver in use, once initialized ( 0 : none)\n( sqpopt_linear_solver_mumps or sqpopt_linear_solver_qdldl ) integer, private :: n = 0 order of the matrix class( sqpopt_sparse_ldl_type ), private, allocatable :: ldl the backend Type-Bound Procedures procedure, public :: initialize => symmetric_solver_initialize procedure, public :: factor => symmetric_solver_factor procedure, public :: solve => symmetric_solver_solve procedure, public :: multiply => symmetric_solver_multiply procedure, public :: destroy => symmetric_solver_destroy Functions public pure function sqpopt_linear_solver_available (solver) result(available) whether the sparse solver solver (a sqpopt_linear_solver_* value)\ncan be used in this build: QDLDL always, MUMPS only in a build with it. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer, intent(in) :: solver the solver ( sqpopt_linear_solver_* ) Return Value logical public pure function sqpopt_linear_solver_name (solver) result(name) the name of the sparse solver solver (a sqpopt_linear_solver_* value). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer, intent(in) :: solver the solver ( sqpopt_linear_solver_* ) Return Value character(len=:), allocatable Subroutines private  subroutine symmetric_solver_initialize (me, n, irow, icol, ok, threads, solver, signs) start the solver for symmetric matrices of order n with the sparsity\npattern irow / icol : each entry stands for itself and (off the\ndiagonal) its mirror image, so give each off-diagonal element in one\ntriangle only; entries given more than once are added together. solver is the backend (a sqpopt_linear_solver_* value; default\nQDLDL). threads is the number of OpenMP threads to factor and solve\nwith (MUMPS only; see sqpopt_mumps_ldl_module ; default 1). signs is the expected sign of each row's pivot (QDLDL only): -1 for the rows\nof a block that should be negative definite (the constraints of a KKT\nmatrix), else +1 or 0 (see delay_negative_rows ). ok is false\nif the solver isn't available in this build, or couldn't be started. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( sqpopt_symmetric_solver_type ), intent(inout) :: me integer, intent(in) :: n order of the matrix integer, intent(in), dimension(:) :: irow row indices of the entries dimension(nnz) integer, intent(in), dimension(:) :: icol column indices of the entries dimension(nnz) logical, intent(out) :: ok whether the solver is ready integer, intent(in), optional :: threads number of OpenMP threads (default 1 ; 0 : as the OpenMP\nenvironment says) integer, intent(in), optional :: solver the sparse solver ( sqpopt_linear_solver_* , default QDLDL) integer, intent(in), optional, dimension(:) :: signs the expected sign of each row's pivot dimension(n) (see above) private  subroutine symmetric_solver_factor (me, val, ok) factor the matrix with the values val (in the order of the pattern\ngiven to symmetric_solver_initialize ). On success, n_negative , n_null , and inertia_known describe its inertia (see the module\ndocumentation), and symmetric_solver_solve can be called. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( sqpopt_symmetric_solver_type ), intent(inout) :: me real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: val values of the entries dimension(nnz) logical, intent(out) :: ok whether the factorization succeeded private  subroutine symmetric_solver_solve (me, b, ok, refine) solve with the factorization of symmetric_solver_factor : b is overwritten by x . The solution is improved by iterative\nrefinement (up to max_refine steps, each one more solve, while the\nresidual is above roundoff and each step at least halves\nit), unless refine is false. ok is false if there is no\nfactorization, the backend reports that the solve failed ( b is then\nunchanged, and out_of_memory is set if an allocation failed), or the\nsolution isn't finite. If a solve of the refinement fails, the\nrefinement stops, and the solution found so far is returned. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( sqpopt_symmetric_solver_type ), intent(inout) :: me real(kind=wp), intent(inout), dimension(:) :: b the right-hand side, overwritten by the solution dimension(n) logical, intent(out) :: ok whether a solution was found logical, intent(in), optional :: refine whether to refine the solution (default .true. ) private  subroutine symmetric_solver_multiply (me, x, y) the product with the matrix last given to symmetric_solver_factor (used for the residuals of the refinement,\nand for testing). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( sqpopt_symmetric_solver_type ), intent(in) :: me real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: x the vector dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(out), dimension(:) :: y the product dimension(n) private  subroutine symmetric_solver_destroy (me) free the solver, and return it to its initial state. It does nothing\nto a solver that was never initialized. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( sqpopt_symmetric_solver_type ), intent(inout) :: me","tags":"","url":"module/sqpopt_symmetric_solver_module.html"},{"title":"sqpopt_convergence_module – sqpopt","text":"Stopping criteria for the SQP algorithm, based on the Karush-Kuhn-Tucker\n(KKT) optimality conditions and feasibility of the constraints and\nbounds, plus the stalled-progress and local-infeasibility tests. Uses sqpopt_types_module sqpopt_kinds sqpopt_linalg_module module~~sqpopt_convergence_module~~UsesGraph module~sqpopt_convergence_module sqpopt_convergence_module module~sqpopt_kinds sqpopt_kinds module~sqpopt_convergence_module->module~sqpopt_kinds module~sqpopt_linalg_module sqpopt_linalg_module module~sqpopt_convergence_module->module~sqpopt_linalg_module module~sqpopt_types_module sqpopt_types_module module~sqpopt_convergence_module->module~sqpopt_types_module iso_fortran_env iso_fortran_env module~sqpopt_kinds->iso_fortran_env module~sqpopt_linalg_module->module~sqpopt_kinds module~sqpopt_linalg_module->module~sqpopt_types_module lusol lusol module~sqpopt_linalg_module->lusol lusol_precision lusol_precision module~sqpopt_linalg_module->lusol_precision module~sqpopt_types_module->module~sqpopt_kinds ieee_arithmetic ieee_arithmetic module~sqpopt_types_module->ieee_arithmetic Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Used by module~~sqpopt_convergence_module~~UsedByGraph module~sqpopt_convergence_module sqpopt_convergence_module module~sqpopt_iterate_module sqpopt_iterate_module module~sqpopt_iterate_module->module~sqpopt_convergence_module module~sqpopt_module sqpopt_module module~sqpopt_module->module~sqpopt_iterate_module module~sqpopt_nlls_module sqpopt_nlls_module module~sqpopt_nlls_module->module~sqpopt_module Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Functions private pure function projected_inf_norm (r, x, x_lb, x_ub, tol) result(res) the infinity norm of the gradient r , projected onto the variable\n bounds: a component only counts if moving along -r would stay feasible\n (free variable, or at a bound but pointing into the interior). Read more… Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: r gradient dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: x current point dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: x_lb variable lower bounds dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: x_ub variable upper bounds dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: tol distance within which a bound counts as active Return Value real(kind=wp) Subroutines public  subroutine check_convergence (x, g, jac, c, x_lb, x_ub, c_lb, c_ub, lambda, ktol, ctol, converged, istat, f, f_prev, x_prev, ftol, xtol, kkt_error, feas_error, viol_prev, dual_inf_tol, f_scale, stat_error) check whether the solver should stop at the current iterate, and why: Read more… Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: x current point dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: g objective gradient at x dimension(n) type( sqpopt_sparse_matrix ), intent(in) :: jac constraint Jacobian at x , dimension(m,n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: c constraint values at x dimension(m) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: x_lb variable lower bounds dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: x_ub variable upper bounds dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: c_lb constraint lower bounds dimension(m) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: c_ub constraint upper bounds dimension(m) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: lambda Lagrange multipliers dimension(m) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: ktol KKT optimality tolerance real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: ctol feasibility tolerance logical, intent(out) :: converged true if the solver should stop at x (see istat for why) integer, intent(out) :: istat the reason: sqpopt_success , sqpopt_stalled , or sqpopt_infeasible if converged , else sqpopt_success (see sqpopt_types_module ) real(kind=wp), intent(in), optional :: f objective value at x (enables the stalled-progress test with f_prev / ftol ) real(kind=wp), intent(in), optional :: f_prev objective value at the previous iterate real(kind=wp), intent(in), optional, dimension(:) :: x_prev the previous iterate dimension(n) (enables the\nstalled-progress test with xtol , and the infeasibility test) real(kind=wp), intent(in), optional :: ftol relative objective-change tolerance for the stalled-progress test real(kind=wp), intent(in), optional :: xtol relative variable-change tolerance for the stalled-progress test real(kind=wp), intent(out), optional :: kkt_error the KKT error: the larger of the stationarity and\ncomplementarity residuals, divided by their scaling\n(so it is directly comparable to ktol ) real(kind=wp), intent(out), optional :: feas_error the largest violation of a constraint or variable bound real(kind=wp), intent(in), optional :: viol_prev the largest constraint violation at x_prev (enables the no-progress condition of the\ninfeasibility test) real(kind=wp), intent(in), optional :: dual_inf_tol if present (with f_scale ), the KKT test\nalso requires the stationarity residual of\nthe unscaled problem to be at most this\n(as IPOPT's dual_inf_tol ) real(kind=wp), intent(in), optional :: f_scale the objective's scale factor: the unscaled\nstationarity residual is the scaled one\ndivided by it real(kind=wp), intent(out), optional :: stat_error the (projected) stationarity residual of the\nscaled problem, without the multiplier scaling","tags":"","url":"module/sqpopt_convergence_module.html"},{"title":"sqpopt_qdldl_ldl_module – sqpopt","text":"The QDLDL backend of sqpopt_symmetric_solver_module ( options%linear_solver = sqpopt_linear_solver_qdldl , the default): QDLDL , a Fortran port\nof the solver of the OSQP QP solver, an fpm dependency, so it\nis always available, in any real kind. It factors in a fill-reducing\norder (approximate minimum degree, with dense rows last), without\npivoting , on one thread, with very little overhead per call. Without pivoting, it is exact for quasi-definite matrices, and for the\nKKT matrices with positive definite once each constraint's row comes after its\nvariables (the signs of qdldl_start , see delay_negative_rows ):\nthe least-squares systems, and the matrices of the quasi-Newton\nHessians. For an indefinite the inertia is still exact as long\nas no pivot is zero (Sylvester's law). Null pivots. A pivot of at most qdldl_zero_tol times the largest\nelement is null. The first one stops a first factorization, which is\nthen repeated with each null pivot replaced by a large one (which makes\nits row and column of the factors negligible, so that a solve returns\none of the solutions of a consistent system, as with MUMPS). Whether\nthose factors are still the matrix's is then checked (see qdldl_faithful ): they are if the matrix is singular, and n_null counts its zero eigenvalues. If they aren't, the zero pivot was an\nartifact of the order (e.g. a Hessian with zeros on its diagonal, which\nwould need a 2x2 pivot), and the inertia is unknown: inertia_known = .false. (what that means for the optimization is\ndecided by sqpopt_kkt_module ). Uses qdldl_module sqpopt_sparse_ldl_module sqpopt_kinds module~~sqpopt_qdldl_ldl_module~~UsesGraph module~sqpopt_qdldl_ldl_module sqpopt_qdldl_ldl_module module~sqpopt_kinds sqpopt_kinds module~sqpopt_qdldl_ldl_module->module~sqpopt_kinds module~sqpopt_sparse_ldl_module sqpopt_sparse_ldl_module module~sqpopt_qdldl_ldl_module->module~sqpopt_sparse_ldl_module qdldl_module qdldl_module module~sqpopt_qdldl_ldl_module->qdldl_module iso_fortran_env iso_fortran_env module~sqpopt_kinds->iso_fortran_env module~sqpopt_sparse_ldl_module->module~sqpopt_kinds Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Used by module~~sqpopt_qdldl_ldl_module~~UsedByGraph module~sqpopt_qdldl_ldl_module sqpopt_qdldl_ldl_module module~sqpopt_symmetric_solver_module sqpopt_symmetric_solver_module module~sqpopt_symmetric_solver_module->module~sqpopt_qdldl_ldl_module module~sqpopt_kkt_module sqpopt_kkt_module module~sqpopt_kkt_module->module~sqpopt_symmetric_solver_module module~sqpopt_module sqpopt_module module~sqpopt_module->module~sqpopt_symmetric_solver_module module~sqpopt_module->module~sqpopt_kkt_module 