t_module module~sqpopt_module->module~sqpopt_least_squares_module module~sqpopt_module->module~sqpopt_iterate_module module~sqpopt_module->module~sqpopt_restoration_module module~sqpopt_module->module~sqpopt_trust_region_module module~sqpopt_module->module~sqpopt_options_module module~sqpopt_module->module~sqpopt_diagnostics_module module~sqpopt_restoration_module->module~sqpopt_least_squares_module module~sqpopt_soc_module->module~sqpopt_least_squares_module module~sqpopt_trust_region_module->module~sqpopt_least_squares_module module~sqpopt_trust_region_module->module~sqpopt_soc_module module~sqpopt_nlls_module sqpopt_nlls_module module~sqpopt_nlls_module->module~sqpopt_module module~sqpopt_nlls_module->module~sqpopt_trust_region_module module~sqpopt_nlls_module->module~sqpopt_options_module module~sqpopt_options_module->module~sqpopt_restoration_module module~sqpopt_diagnostics_module->module~sqpopt_options_module Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial real(kind=wp), private, parameter :: reg = 1.0e-8_wp the regularization , relative to the square of the\nlargest element of the Jacobian's rows and columns in use (it limits the relative accuracy of\nthe solution of a consistent system to about this) Derived Types type, public :: sqpopt_least_squares_type the direct least-squares solver of one solve (see the module\ndocumentation). It holds a sparse solver, so it must not be copied,\nand least_squares_destroy must be called when the solve ends. Components Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial logical, public :: enabled = .false. whether it can be used (set by least_squares_initialize , and\nunset if a factorization fails) type( sqpopt_kkt_type ), public :: kkt the matrix above, as the KKT matrix of the Hessian I (see sqpopt_kkt_module ; public for its solver's counts and time) integer, private :: n = 0 number of variables integer, private :: m = 0 number of constraints Type-Bound Procedures procedure, public :: initialize => least_squares_initialize procedure, public :: new_matrices => least_squares_new_matrices procedure, public :: min_norm => least_squares_min_norm procedure, public :: multipliers => least_squares_multipliers procedure, public :: destroy => least_squares_destroy Functions private pure function regularization (jac, rows, free) result(eps) the regularization for the rows S of the Jacobian jac selected by rows (in the variables selected by free , if\ngiven): reg times the square of the largest element of that part of\nthe Jacobian, or reg itself if the part is zero. Only the elements\nthat the solve uses count, so that neither small elements are swamped\nby the regularization, nor a large element of another row (or of a\nvariable at a bound) makes it too large for the rows of S . Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( sqpopt_sparse_matrix ), intent(in) :: jac the constraint Jacobian dimension(m,n) logical, intent(in), dimension(:) :: rows whether each row is in S dimension(m) logical, intent(in), optional, dimension(:) :: free whether each variable is used dimension(n) (default:\nall are) Return Value real(kind=wp) the regularization Subroutines private  subroutine least_squares_initialize (me, n, m, jac_irow, jac_icol, ok, threads, solver) start the solver for a problem with n variables, m constraints, and\nthe Jacobian sparsity pattern jac_irow / jac_icol , with the sparse\nsolver solver (see kkt_initialize ). ok is false, and the solver\nis left disabled, if that sparse solver isn't available in this build or\ncouldn't be started. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( sqpopt_least_squares_type ), intent(inout) :: me integer, intent(in) :: n number of variables integer, intent(in) :: m number of constraints integer, intent(in), dimension(:) :: jac_irow row indices of the Jacobian's nonzeros integer, intent(in), dimension(:) :: jac_icol column indices of the Jacobian's nonzeros logical, intent(out) :: ok whether the solver is now enabled integer, intent(in), optional :: threads number of OpenMP threads of the sparse solver (see symmetric_solver_initialize ) integer, intent(in), optional :: solver the sparse solver ( sqpopt_linear_solver_* , default auto ) private  subroutine least_squares_new_matrices (me) note that the Jacobian's values have changed (a new major iteration). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( sqpopt_least_squares_type ), intent(inout) :: me private  subroutine least_squares_min_norm (me, jac, rows, r, d, ok) the (regularized) minimum-norm solution d of (see the\nmodule documentation), for the rows S of jac selected by rows . ok is false if the solver isn't enabled, or the factorization or the\nsolve failed (the solver is then disabled for the rest of the solve):\nthe caller then falls back on its iterative method. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( sqpopt_least_squares_type ), intent(inout) :: me type( sqpopt_sparse_matrix ), intent(in) :: jac the constraint Jacobian dimension(m,n) logical, intent(in), dimension(:) :: rows whether each row is in S dimension(m) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: r the right-hand side (used for the rows in S ) dimension(m) real(kind=wp), intent(out), dimension(:) :: d the solution dimension(n) logical, intent(out) :: ok whether d was computed private  subroutine least_squares_multipliers (me, jac, rows, free, g, lambda, ok) the (regularized) least-squares multipliers of the rows S of jac selected by rows , in the variables selected by free : , from the system\nof the module documentation with the right-hand side . lambda is only set for the rows in S . ok is false if the solver\nisn't enabled, or the factorization or the solve failed (the solver is\nthen disabled for the rest of the solve). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( sqpopt_least_squares_type ), intent(inout) :: me type( sqpopt_sparse_matrix ), intent(in) :: jac the constraint Jacobian dimension(m,n) logical, intent(in), dimension(:) :: rows whether each row is in S dimension(m) logical, intent(in), dimension(:) :: free whether each variable is free (not at a bound) dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: g the objective gradient dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(inout), dimension(:) :: lambda the multipliers dimension(m) (set for the rows in S ) logical, intent(out) :: ok whether they were computed public  subroutine multiplier_estimate (jac, rows, free, g, lambda, ok, least_squares) a first-order estimate of the constraint multipliers at a point: the\nleast-squares solution of the stationarity condition Read more… Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( sqpopt_sparse_matrix ), intent(in) :: jac the constraint Jacobian dimension(m,n) logical, intent(in), dimension(:) :: rows whether each row is in S dimension(m) logical, intent(in), dimension(:) :: free whether each variable is free (not at a bound) dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: g the objective gradient dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(inout), dimension(:) :: lambda the multipliers dimension(m) (set for the rows in S ) logical, intent(out) :: ok whether they were computed type( sqpopt_least_squares_type ), intent(inout), optional :: least_squares the direct least-squares solver private  subroutine least_squares_destroy (me) free the solver, and return to the initial (disabled) state. It does\nnothing if the solver was never started. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( sqpopt_least_squares_type ), intent(inout) :: me","tags":"","url":"module/sqpopt_least_squares_module.html"},{"title":"sqpopt_mumps_ldl_module – sqpopt","text":"The MUMPS backend of sqpopt_symmetric_solver_module ( options%linear_solver = sqpopt_linear_solver_mumps ): MUMPS , a multifrontal factorization with pivoting and OpenMP threads. Its inertia is always\nknown ( icntl(13) = 1 makes the count of negative pivots exact), and a\nsingular matrix's null pivots are detected ( icntl(24) = 1 ). This is the library's only interface to MUMPS, and the only source file\nthat depends on the HAS_MUMPS preprocessor directive: without it, sqpopt_has_mumps is false and mumps_start always fails. With it,\nthe library must be linked with the sequential, double precision MUMPS\nlibrary, and compiled in double precision (the default kind, see sqpopt_kinds ). Threads. MUMPS and the BLAS under it can use OpenMP threads, if they\nwere built with OpenMP (conda-forge's mumps-seq is): the threads argument of mumps_start , 1 by default, or 0 to leave it to the\nOpenMP environment ( OMP_NUM_THREADS , or every core). On small matrices\nthe threads cost far more time than they save (the 305\nHock-Schittkowski problems took 24.5 s with 10 threads, and 1.4 s with\none). Whether more threads help a large matrix depends on its factors:\nthe threads work inside the dense blocks of the factorization (its\nfrontal matrices), so they need those to be large. Measured (analysis,\ntwo factorizations, and a solve; 1, 2, 4, and 8 threads): a 3-D grid\nmatrix of order 216,000 took 11.5, 7.0, 4.9, and 4.8 s; a 2-D grid\nmatrix of order 490,000 took 2.0 s with any number; and the banded KKT\nmatrices of example/benchmark_large.f90 gained nothing either. MUMPS\nsets the number of threads when it is called and restores it when it\nreturns, so the rest of the program is not affected. Uses sqpopt_sparse_ldl_module sqpopt_kinds module~~sqpopt_mumps_ldl_module~~UsesGraph module~sqpopt_mumps_ldl_module sqpopt_mumps_ldl_module module~sqpopt_kinds sqpopt_kinds module~sqpopt_mumps_ldl_module->module~sqpopt_kinds module~sqpopt_sparse_ldl_module sqpopt_sparse_ldl_module module~sqpopt_