ules which it uses. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, public, parameter :: sqpopt_restoration_phase = 1 (default) a feasibility restoration phase\n(see the module documentation) integer, public, parameter :: sqpopt_restoration_gauss_newton = 2 a single Gauss-Newton step on the violation real(kind=wp), private, parameter :: zeta = 1.0_wp weight of the feasibility QP's proximal term (only its ratio\nto the QP's elastic weight matters: with consistent linearized constraints\nthe step is the one closest to the proximal center) Derived Types type, public :: sqpopt_restoration_type the state of the feasibility restoration phase (internal; reset on each solve ) Components Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial logical, public :: active = .false. whether the solver is in the restoration phase integer, public :: n_iter = 0 number of iterations of the current phase integer, public :: n_phases = 0 number of restoration phases so far real(kind=wp), public :: theta_ref = 0.0_wp violation where the phase started real(kind=wp), public, dimension(:), allocatable :: x_ref the point where the phase started (the proximal center) type( sqpopt_qp_solver_type ), public :: qp the QP solver for the feasibility QPs (a copy of the main one,\nso that the latter's warm start is kept for the optimality QPs) type( sqpopt_hessian_type ), public :: hess the feasibility QP's Hessian (the proximal term, ) Type-Bound Procedures procedure, public :: enter => restoration_phase_enter procedure, public :: step => restoration_phase_step procedure, public :: done => restoration_phase_done Functions private  function restoration_phase_done (me, linesearch, theta, f, exit_factor, feas_tol, max_iter) result(done) whether the restoration phase can end at a point with violation theta and objective f : the violation is at most exit_factor times its value where the phase started and the point is\nacceptable to the filter or funnel (see sqpopt_linesearch_type 's globalization_acceptable ); or the point is feasible\n( feas_tol ); or the phase has taken max_iter iterations. Ends the phase ( active=.false. ) if so. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( sqpopt_restoration_type ), intent(inout) :: me type( sqpopt_linesearch_type ), intent(in) :: linesearch supplies the filter or funnel real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: theta violation at the new point real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: f objective at the new point real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: exit_factor required reduction of the violation real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: feas_tol a violation this small ends the phase regardless integer, intent(in) :: max_iter maximum number of iterations of a phase Return Value logical private elemental function violation (c, c_lb, c_ub) result(rc) the signed violation of the constraint bounds: c-c_lb below the lower\nbound, c-c_ub above the upper bound, and 0 in between. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: c constraint value real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: c_lb its lower bound real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: c_ub its upper bound Return Value real(kind=wp) Subroutines public  subroutine restoration_step (problem, jac, x, c, max_step, x_new, alpha, istat, direction, least_squares) take one Gauss-Newton step toward feasibility of the constraints (see\nthe module-level documentation), with a backtracking Armijo search on . If no decrease is found, x_new=x and istat=sqpopt_line_search_failed (also if LSQR stops at its\niteration limit before finding the Gauss-Newton step). With least_squares (if it is enabled), the Gauss-Newton step is computed by\na direct solve, and by LSQR only if that fails. Read more… Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( sqpopt_problem_type ), intent(inout) :: problem problem definition type( sqpopt_sparse_matrix ), intent(in) :: jac constraint Jacobian at x , dimension(m,n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: x current point dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: c constraint values at x dimension(m) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: max_step cap on real(kind=wp), intent(out), dimension(:) :: x_new new point dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(out) :: alpha accepted step length ( 0 if none) integer, intent(out) :: istat status code (see sqpopt_types_module ) real(kind=wp), intent(in), optional, dimension(:) :: direction search direction to use instead of Gauss-Newton dimension(n) type( sqpopt_least_squares_type ), intent(inout), optional :: least_squares the direct least-squares solver\n(see sqpopt_least_squares_module ) public  subroutine escape_step (problem, jac, x, c, tol, x_new, istat) look for a second-order decrease of the constraint violation from a point x that is stationary for it (where check_convergence would report sqpopt_infeasible ). Such a point may be a saddle of the violation rather\nthan a minimum: e.g. on a symmetry plane of the problem ( x_j=0 , with\nevery function even in x_j ), where the gradients' x_j components all\nvanish, so no first-order method, and no exactly computed step, ever\nleaves the plane. Read more… Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( sqpopt_problem_type ), intent(inout) :: problem problem definition type( sqpopt_sparse_matrix ), intent(in) :: jac constraint Jacobian at x , dimension(m,n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: x current point dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: c constraint values at x dimension(m) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: tol a Jacobian column is negligible below this (the convergence\ntest's ktol ) real(kind=wp), intent(out), dimension(:) :: x_new new point dimension(n) integer, intent(out) :: istat status code (see sqpopt_types_module ) private  subroutine restoration_phase_enter (me, x, theta, qp_solver) start a feasibility restoration phase at x (with violation theta ): record where it started, and set up the feasibility\nQP's solver (a copy of qp_solver ) and Hessian. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( sqpopt_restoration_type ), intent(inout) :: me real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: x the current point dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: theta its constraint violation type( sqpopt_qp_solver_type ), intent(in) :: qp_solver the main QP solver (copied) private  subroutine restoration_phase_step (me, problem, jac, x, c, x_new, alpha, istat) one iteration of the feasibility restoration phase from x : solve the\nfeasibility QP Read more… Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( sqpopt_restoration_type ), intent(inout) :: me type( sqpopt_problem_type ), intent(inout) :: problem problem definition type( sqpopt_sparse_matrix ), intent(in) :: jac constraint Jacobian at x , dimension(m,n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: x current point dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: c constraint values at x dimension(m) real(kind=wp), intent(out), dimension(:) :: x_new new point dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(out) :: alpha accepted step length ( 0 if none) integer, intent(out) :: istat status code (see sqpopt_types_module )","tags":"","url":"module/sqpopt_restoration_module.html"},{"title":"sqpopt_problem_module – sqpopt","text":"Defines the nonlinear program (NLP) that is to be solved: subject to: where is a vector of nonlinear constraints. An equality\nconstraint is specified by setting the corresponding elements of and to the same value, and an inequality\nconstraint by setting them to different values (using for one-sided inequalities). Variable bounds are specified similarly\nvia and . The Jacobian of and the Hessian of the Lagrangian are\nnever treated as dense or arrays:\ntheir (fixed) sparsity patterns are supplied once as 1-based COO irow / icol triplets (the same convention used by the lusol and LSQR dependencies), and the user-supplied evaluation\nroutines only need to fill in the corresponding nonzero values on\neach call. User functions. The problem is defined by two routines (see set_functions ): fc , which evaluates the objective and\nthe constraints together, and gjac , which evaluates the\nobjective gradient and the nonzero values of the\nconstraint Jacobian together (and, optionally, hess for an exact\nHessian of the Lagrangian). Every user function has two trailing\narguments: status ( integer, intent(inout) ): 0 on entry. Leave it 0 on\n  success; set it > 0 if the function cannot be evaluated at x (e.g.\n  a domain error) -- the solver then treats the point like one where\n  the function returned NaN, and backs off from it -- or < 0 to ask the\n  solver to stop ( istat=sqpopt_user_requested_stop ). data ( class(*), intent(inout), optional ): the user data object\n  given to set_functions (absent if none was given), for passing any\n  context to the functions without module variables; use select type to access it. Evaluation layer. The solver evaluates everything through the\ntype-bound [[eval_f_cached|f]], [[eval_c_cached|c]], [[eval_g_cached|g]],\nand [[eval_jac_cached|jac]] methods, which handle status / data , count\nthe user calls, keep a small cache of recent evaluations (so e.g. the\npoint accepted by a line search is not evaluated again, and asking for c at a point where f was just evaluated doesn't call fc again),\nand apply the objective and constraint scaling (see compute_scaling ): the solver works with and . Uses sqpopt_types_module sqpopt_kinds ieee_arithmetic module~~sqpopt_problem_module~~UsesGraph module~sqpopt_problem_module sqpopt_problem_module ieee_arithmetic ieee_arithmetic module~sqpopt_problem_module->ieee_arithmetic module~sqpopt_kinds sqpopt_kinds module~sqpopt_problem_module->module~sqpopt_kinds module~sqpopt_types_module sqpopt_types_module module~sqpopt_problem_module->module~sqpopt_types_module iso_fortran_env iso_fortran_env module~sqpopt_kinds->iso_fortran_env module~sqpopt_types_module->ieee_arithmetic module~sqpopt_types_module->module~sqpopt_kinds Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Su