tion of the working set, with sparse\nLU factors of the basis (SQOPT-style, see the module docs) integer, public, parameter :: sqpopt_null_space_lsqr = 2 orthogonal projections onto the null space, by LSQR Derived Types type, public :: sqpopt_reduced_hessian_qp_type options for the sparse (projected-CG) active-set QP solver. Components Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, public :: null_space = sqpopt_null_space_lu how the null space of the working set is handled\n(see the sqpopt_null_space_* constants) integer, public :: max_iter = 100 minimum limit on the number of active-set iterations per QP solve\n(the actual limit is max(max_iter, 10*(number of rows+1)) ) integer, public :: max_pcg_iter = 0 maximum projected-CG iterations per active-set face\n( <=0 => twice the number of unknowns) integer, public :: dense_max_ns = 50 with null_space=sqpopt_null_space_lu , a face with at most this\nmany superbasics is solved exactly, with a dense Cholesky\nfactorization of the reduced Hessian, instead of by CG\n(if it is positive definite; 0 = always CG) real(kind=wp), public :: active_tol = 1.0e-8_wp relative tolerance for a row being at a bound, and for the sign\nof a multiplier (relative to the largest multiplier) real(kind=wp), public :: opt_tol = 1.0e-10_wp relative tolerance on the projected-gradient stationarity test\n(relative to ) real(kind=wp), public :: pcg_rtol = 1.0e-10_wp projected CG stops once the projected residual has been reduced\nby this factor (or meets opt_tol ) real(kind=wp), public :: feas_tol = 1.0e-6_wp an elastic slack larger than feas_tol*max(1,|bound|) at the\nsolution counts as a violated linearized constraint real(kind=wp), public :: elastic_weight = 1.0e4_wp initial elastic penalty weight, relative to real(kind=wp), public :: elastic_weight_max = 1.0e8_wp largest elastic penalty weight tried (same scaling) before the\nlinearized constraints are declared inconsistent (lower than\nthe dense solver's, since the iterative projections' accuracy\nis relative to the penalty weight) real(kind=wp), public :: lsqr_atol = 0.0_wp LSQR relative error tolerance in A (0 => LSQR default) real(kind=wp), public :: lsqr_btol = 0.0_wp LSQR relative error tolerance in b (0 => LSQR default) real(kind=wp), public :: lsqr_conlim = 0.0_wp LSQR upper limit on cond(Abar) (0 => LSQR default) integer, public :: lsqr_itnlim = 0 LSQR maximum iterations per solve ( <=0 => 2*(rows+columns)+10 ) logical, public :: warm_start = .true. start from the previous solve's final working set, if the problem\nsize is unchanged (see sqpopt_qp_dense_module : the crash and\nwarm starts work the same way here, with LSQR computing the\nminimum-norm starting step) integer, public, dimension(:), allocatable :: force_sign real(kind=wp), public :: force_weight = 0.0_wp integer, public :: n_iter = 0 number of active-set iterations taken by the last solve (output) integer, public :: n_working = 0 number of general rows and variable bounds in the final working set of the\nlast solve (output) integer, public :: n_slacks = 0 number of elastic slacks in the last solve (output; see the module docs) logical, public :: negative_curvature = .false. whether the last solve found a direction of negative curvature\nof the Hessian in the variables (output; the QP was nonconvex) logical, public :: keep_slacks = .false. whether to keep the elastic slacks of each solve in slack_row and slack_value (internal input, set by the solver for its diagnostics:\nsee options%diagnostic_level ) integer, public, dimension(:), allocatable :: slack_row with keep_slacks : the general row of each elastic\nslack of the last solve (output) real(kind=wp), public, dimension(:), allocatable :: slack_value and its value at the solution: by how much the step\nviolates that linearized constraint (output) integer, public, dimension(:), allocatable :: warm_status side (-1/0/+1) of each general row and variable bound Type-Bound Procedures procedure, public :: solve => solve_reduced_hessian_qp type, private :: csr_rows the combined constraint rows, in compressed-row form Components Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, public :: nrows = 0 integer, public :: ncols = 0 integer, public, dimension(:), allocatable :: ptr row k is entries ptr(k):ptr(k+1)-1 integer, public, dimension(:), allocatable :: col real(kind=wp), public, dimension(:), allocatable :: val Functions private pure function row_dot (rows, k, v) result(s) dot product of row k of rows with v . Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( csr_rows ), intent(in) :: rows the rows, in compressed-row form integer, intent(in) :: k index of the row real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: v the vector Return Value real(kind=wp) private pure function row_norm (rows, k) result(s) 2-norm of row k of rows . Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( csr_rows ), intent(in) :: rows the rows, in compressed-row form integer, intent(in) :: k index of the row Return Value real(kind=wp) Subroutines private  subroutine solve_reduced_hessian_qp (me, hessian, jac, x, g, c, x_lb, x_ub, c_lb, c_ub, p, lambda, istat) solve the linearized QP subproblem for the search direction p and\nthe associated Lagrange multipliers lambda (see the module-level\ndocumentation). istat is as for sqpopt_qp_dense_module 's solver. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( sqpopt_reduced_hessian_qp_type ), intent(inout) :: me type( sqpopt_hessian_type ), intent(inout) :: hessian matrix-free Hessian approximation type( sqpopt_sparse_matrix ), intent(in) :: jac sparse constraint Jacobian, dimension(m,n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: x current point dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: g objective gradient dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: c constraint values dimension(m) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: x_lb variable bounds dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: x_ub variable bounds dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: c_lb constraint bounds dimension(m) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: c_ub constraint bounds dimension(m) real(kind=wp), intent(out), dimension(:) :: p search direction dimension(n) real(kind=wp), intent(out), dimension(:) :: lambda Lagrange multiplier estimate dimension(m) integer, intent(out) :: istat status code (see sqpopt_types_module ) private  subroutine basis_triplets (cptr, crow, cval, bvar, bi, bj, bv) the basis matrix B as COO triplets: the columns bvar of the matrix\nstored by columns ( cptr , crow , cval ), numbered in that order. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer, intent(in), dimension(:) :: cptr start of each column in crow / cval integer, intent(in), dimension(:) :: crow row index of each nonzero real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: cval value of each nonzero integer, intent(in), dimension(:) :: bvar the basic columns integer, intent(out), dimension(:), allocatable :: bi row indices of the nonzeros of B integer, intent(out), dimension(:), allocatable :: bj their column indices in B real(kind=wp), intent(out), dimension(:), allocatable :: bv their values private  subroutine choose_basis (m, nn, nx, cptr, crow, cval, state, is_eqv, chosen, istat) pick a basis ( m linearly independent columns) of the m x nn sparse\nmatrix A = [J E -I] (by columns: cptr , crow , cval ; the last nn-nx columns are the row slacks -I ), for the basis method of sqpopt_reduced_hessian_qp_type , by one rank-revealing LU (see independent_columns ). Read more… Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer, intent(in) :: m number of rows integer, intent(in) :: nn number of columns (unknowns) integer, intent(in) :: nx number of columns before the row slacks integer, intent(in), dimension(:) :: cptr start of each column in crow / cval dimension(nn+1) integer, intent(in), dimension(:) :: crow row index of each nonzero real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: cval value of each nonzero integer, intent(in), dimension(:) :: state 0 = free, -1/+1 = fixed at a bound (a candidate) logical, intent(in), dimension(:) :: is_eqv unknowns with equal bounds logical, intent(out), dimension(:) :: chosen the basic columns integer, intent(out) :: istat 0 if exactly m columns were picked private  subroutine build_working_set (rows, status, m, ja, fixed, orig_idx, n_active) the working set, split into its active variable-bound rows -- which\nsimply fix those unknowns ( fixed ) -- and its active general rows,\ngathered (renumbered 1..ja%nrows ) into the COO matrix ja restricted\nto the free unknowns (entries in fixed columns are dropped). Handling\nthe bounds this way, rather than as rows in ja , keeps the least-squares\nproblems small and makes the fixed coordinates of every projection\nexactly zero. orig_idx gives the index in rows of each active row:\nthe general rows first (in the order of ja 's rows), then the bounds. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( csr_rows ), intent(in) :: rows all the rows: the general constraints, then the bounds on each unknown integer, intent(in), dimension(:) :: status 0 = inactive, -1/+1 = active at the lower/upper bound integer, intent(in) :: m number of general rows (the rest are the bounds on each unknown) type( sqpopt_sparse_matrix ), intent(out) :: ja the active general rows, on the free unknowns logical, intent(out), dimension(:), allocatable :: fixed dimension(ncols) : unknowns fixed at a bound integer, intent(out), dimension(:), allocatable :: orig_idx index in rows of each active row integer, intent(out) :: n_active number of active rows","tags":"","url":"module/sqpopt_qp_reduced_hessian_module.html"},{"title":"sqpopt_nlls_module – sqpopt","text":"Nonlinear least-squares problems, solved with the SQP solver\n( sqpopt_nlls_type ): an optional layer on top of sqpopt_type ,\nwhich the rest of the library doesn't know about. The problem is for residuals (e.g. the differences between a model and\ndata) and, optionally, constraints . It is solved in the form\nproposed by Schittkowski (the code DFNLP): with a new variable for each residual, which is an ordinary nonlinear program for sqpopt_type , in variables and constraints. Its QP subproblems\nare always consistent as far as the residuals go (any step in has a to go with it), and their step is a Gauss-Newton step\nwith a correction from the Hessian approximation, as in the special\ncodes for least-squares problems. Use it for data fitting, and for a system of equations that can't\nall hold (more equations than unknowns): given as equality constraints\nto sqpopt_type they make every QP subproblem inconsistent, and the\nsolve ends as sqpopt_infeasible at best, after many restoration\nsteps. The Hessian. With the quasi-Newton modes of options%hessian_mode (the default), the solver approximates the Hessian of the transformed\nproblem as it does any other. With sqpopt_hessian_exact , this module\nsupplies the Gauss-Newton Hessian: the identity on , and zero\non (the second derivatives of the residuals and of the\nconstraints are neglected, as in the Gauss-Newton method). The solver's\nHessian shift then acts as the damping of the Levenberg-Marquardt\nmethod. It usually takes fewer iterations, mostly so when the residuals\nare small at the solution, but it fails more often. What to expect. On 94 overdetermined systems of equations from\nCUTEst (at most 250 iterations), the default L-BFGS reached the best\nsum of squares found on 59, with 6,842 residual evaluations in all, and\nthe Gauss-Newton Hessian on 68, with 13,154 (one or the other: 79).\nGiven to sqpopt_type as equality constraints: 52, with 52,958. A\ncode written for least squares does better on such problems (scipy's least_squares : 87, with 10,326), so prefer one (e.g. MINPACK) for an\nunconstrained fit that is hard. What this interface adds is the\nsolver's constraints and sparsity. It is a layer on the solver, not a\nleast-squares method of its own: the solver is unchanged by it. Uses sqpopt_prob