on(l) real(kind=wp), intent(out), optional :: sum_of_squares real(kind=wp), intent(out), optional, dimension(:) :: lambda constraint multipliers dimension(m) private  subroutine nlls_get_results (me, results) the results of the last solve , as the SQP solver returns them for the transformed problem (see the module documentation): x is the\nvariables followed by , f is , and c and lambda are those of the residual equations followed by the\nconstraints. The status, the counts, and the times are as for any solve\n( n_eval_fc and n_eval_gjac count the calls of residuals and jacobian ). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( sqpopt_nlls_type ), intent(in) :: me type( sqpopt_results_type ), intent(out) :: results the results of the last solve private  subroutine nlls_destroy (me) free everything. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( sqpopt_nlls_type ), intent(inout) :: me private  subroutine nlls_fc (x, f, c, status, data) the objective and the constraints and of the transformed problem, for the solver. data is the\nwrapper's state ( nlls_state ). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: x the variables, then z dimension(n+l) real(kind=wp), intent(out) :: f real(kind=wp), intent(out), dimension(:) :: c , then dimension(l+m) integer, intent(inout) :: status 0 on entry; the user function's status on exit class(*), intent(inout), optional :: data the wrapper's state ( nlls_state ) private  subroutine nlls_gjac (x, g, jac_val, accuracy, status, data) the objective gradient and the Jacobian's nonzeros (the\nresiduals' Jacobian, -1 for each , then the constraints'\nJacobian) of the transformed problem, for the solver. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: x the variables, then z dimension(n+l) real(kind=wp), intent(out), dimension(:) :: g objective gradient dimension(n+l) real(kind=wp), intent(out), dimension(:) :: jac_val nonzeros of the Jacobian (see above) integer, intent(in) :: accuracy requested accuracy, passed on to the user's function integer, intent(inout) :: status 0 on entry; the user function's status on exit class(*), intent(inout), optional :: data the wrapper's state ( nlls_state ) private  subroutine nlls_hess (x, lambda, hess_val, status, data) the Gauss-Newton Hessian of the transformed problem, for the solver (with options%hessian_mode = sqpopt_hessian_exact ): the identity on .\nIts pattern is the diagonal elements of . Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: x the variables, then z dimension(n+l) (unused) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: lambda multipliers dimension(l+m) (unused: the residuals'\nsecond derivatives are neglected) real(kind=wp), intent(out), dimension(:) :: hess_val the Hessian's nonzeros dimension(l) integer, intent(inout) :: status 0 on entry (left unchanged) class(*), intent(inout), optional :: data the wrapper's state ( nlls_state ) (unused)","tags":"","url":"module/sqpopt_nlls_module.html"},{"title":"sqpopt_hessian_module – sqpopt","text":"Limited-memory quasi-Newton approximation to the Hessian of the\nLagrangian. Rather than storing a dense matrix, only\nthe last max_history step/gradient-change vector pairs are kept ( max_history is a small constant, independent of n ), and\nHessian(-inverse)-vector products are formed matrix-free using the\ncompact representations and the standard two-loop recursion. Supports\n(Powell-damped) BFGS and SR1 updates. The pairs are kept in a circular buffer (no data is moved when the\noldest pair is discarded), together with their inner products and , which are updated in O(nk) as each pair is\nadded. The small 2k x 2k (BFGS) or k x k (SR1) middle matrix of the\ncompact representation is formed from them and LU-factored only when\nthe pairs or the scaling change, not on every product, so each hessian_vector_product costs only O(nk) . The diagonal (see hessian_diagonal ) is likewise kept until the pairs or the scaling\nchange. The dense QP solver, which needs the whole matrix, gets it from hessian_dense . Exact mode (see hessian_set_exact ): instead of the quasi-Newton\napproximation, the user's sparse Hessian of the Lagrangian is used,\nwith the values set at each major iteration by hessian_set_values .\nThe products are formed from its nonzeros, plus a shift ( ), a simple primal inertia\ncorrection for an indefinite Hessian (as in IPOPT and Uno, but without\nfactorizing): wherever the solver would restart a quasi-Newton\napproximation ( hessian_reset : the QP step was not a descent\ndirection, a step failed, or a run of steps was very short), is increased tenfold, from shift_min times the size of the Hessian's\nlargest element; at each new major iteration that follows a good step,\nit is divided by 3 (and set to 0 once below that minimum). The quasi-Newton updates do nothing\nin this mode. With options%inertia_control , the shift that an\niteration needs is instead found from a factorization (see sqpopt_inertia_module ), and only the increases after a failed step\nor a run of very short ones are carried over to the next iteration. The exact Hessian depends on the multipliers it is evaluated with. After\na step whose shift was large ( shift_dominant ), the QP's multipliers\nmostly balance the shift, so sqpopt_iterate replaces them by a\nleast-squares estimate before evaluating the next Hessian (see multiplier_estimate ). For the sparse factorizations (see sqpopt_kkt_module ), the\nquasi-Newton matrix is available in its compact form, a multiple of the\nidentity plus a matrix of low rank r : with , (BFGS, r = 2k ) or , (SR1, r = k ), and the middle\nmatrix of factor_middle_matrix : see hessian_low_rank_size , hessian_low_rank_multiply , hessian_low_rank_column , and hessian_low_rank_middle . The shift also applies to the quasi-Newton matrices (it is zero\nunless the inertia control sets it, for SR1). Uses sqpopt_kinds sqpopt_dense_linalg_module module~~sqpopt_hessian_module~~UsesGraph module~sqpopt_hessian_module sqpopt_hessian_module module~sqpopt_dense_linalg_module sqpopt_dense_linalg_module module~sqpopt_hessian_module->module~sqpopt_dense_linalg_module module~sqpopt_kinds sqpopt_kinds module~sqpopt_hessian_module->module~sqpopt_kinds module~sqpopt_dense_linalg_module->module~sqpopt_kinds iso_fortran_env iso_fortran_env module~sqpopt_kinds->iso_fortran_env Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Used by module~~sqpopt_hessian_module~~UsedByGraph module~sqpopt_hessian_module sqpopt_hessian_module module~sqpopt_inertia_module sqpopt_inertia_module module~sqpopt_inertia_module->module~sqpopt_hessian_module module~sqpopt_kkt_module sqpopt_kkt_module module~sqpopt_inertia_module->module~sqpopt_kkt_module module~sqpopt_iterate_module sqpopt_iterate_module module~sqpopt_iterate_module->module~sqpopt_hessian_module module~sqpopt_iterate_module->module~sqpopt_inertia_module module~sqpopt_iterate_module->module~sqpopt_kkt_module module~sqpopt_options_module sqpopt_options_module module~sqpopt_iterate_module->module~sqpopt_options_module module~sqpopt_qp_direct_module sqpopt_qp_direct_module module~sqpopt_iterate_module->module~sqpopt_qp_direct_module module~sqpopt_qp_solver_module sqpopt_qp_solver_module module~sqpopt_iterate_module->module~sqpopt_qp_solver_module module~sqpopt_restoration_module sqpopt_restoration_module module~sqpopt_iterate_module->module~sqpopt_restoration_module module~sqpopt_trust_region_module sqpopt_trust_region_module module~sqpopt_iterate_module->module~sqpopt_trust_region_module module~sqpopt_diagnostics_module sqpopt_diagnostics_module module~sqpopt_itera